window.MICRO_CHAPTERS = [{"id":1,"title":"Economic Issues and Concepts","topics":[{"id":"micro-c1-t1","title":"1.1 What Is Economics?","body":"**Economics** is the study of how scarce resources are used to satisfy unlimited human wants. The main **factors of production** are land, labour, and capital. Goods are tangible products, while services are intangible.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t2","title":"Scarcity and Choice","body":"Resources are limited relative to human wants.\n- Scarcity means society cannot produce everything people want.\n- Therefore, scarcity forces people, firms, and governments to make **choices**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t3","title":"Opportunity Cost","body":"**Opportunity cost** is the value of the **next-best alternative forgone** when a choice is made.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t4","title":"Budget Line","body":"A budget line shows the combinations of two goods that can be purchased with a fixed amount of resources.\n\n- Points **on or inside** the line are attainable.\n- Points **outside** the line are unattainable.\n- Moving along the line shows the opportunity cost of obtaining more of one good.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch1-page8.png","alt":"Budget Line: course diagram from Chapter 1, page 8","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 1, page 8"}]},{"id":"micro-c1-t5","title":"Production Possibilities Boundary — PPB","body":"A **Production Possibilities Boundary (PPB)** shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce using its available resources and technology.\n\nIt demonstrates:\n- **Scarcity**\n- **Choice**\n- **Opportunity cost**","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t6","title":"Reading the PPB","body":"**On the PPB:** attainable and productively efficient.\n- **Inside the PPB:** attainable but inefficient.\n- **Outside the PPB:** currently unattainable.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch1-page10.png","alt":"Reading the PPB: course diagram from Chapter 1, page 10","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 1, page 10"}]},{"id":"micro-c1-t7","title":"Economic Growth","body":"Economic growth increases an economy's productive capacity.\n\n- The PPB shifts **outward**.\n- Goods that were once unattainable may become attainable.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch1-page14.png","alt":"Economic Growth: course diagram from Chapter 1, page 14","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 1, page 14"}]},{"id":"micro-c1-t8","title":"1. What Is Produced and How?","body":"Economies must decide:\n- What goods and services to produce.\n- How resources will be allocated.\n- Whether government should influence production.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t9","title":"2. What Is Consumed and by Whom?","body":"Economies must determine how total production is distributed among people.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t10","title":"3. Why Are Resources Sometimes Idle?","body":"If resources are unemployed or unused, the economy operates **inside its PPB** rather than on it.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t11","title":"4. Is Productive Capacity Growing?","body":"Growth allows an economy to produce more goods and services over time.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t12","title":"Microeconomics","body":"Studies:\n- Individual consumers.\n- Firms.\n- Markets.\n- Resource allocation.\n- The price system.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t13","title":"Macroeconomics","body":"Studies economy-wide totals such as:\n- Total output.\n- Employment.\n- Economic growth.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t14","title":"Self-Organizing Market Economy","body":"Consumers and producers independently pursue their own interests, yet their choices can combine into an organized market outcome.\n\n**Efficiency** means organizing resources to produce the goods and services people want using the least possible resources.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t15","title":"Incentives and Self-Interest","body":"Individuals generally respond to incentives.\n- Sellers tend to offer more when prices are high.\n- Buyers tend to purchase more when prices are low.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t16","title":"Main Decision Makers","body":"1. Consumers\n2. Producers\n3. Government","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t17","title":"Specialization and Trade","body":"**Specialization of labour:** workers focus on particular goods or services.\n- **Division of labour:** production is divided into separate specialized tasks. \n- Money makes trade easier by avoiding the problems of barter.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t18","title":"Globalization","body":"Globalization refers to the increased importance of international trade.\n\nMajor causes:\n- Lower transportation costs.\n- Advances in information technology.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c1-t19","title":"1.3 Economic Systems","body":"Three pure types:\n- **Traditional economy**\n- **Command economy**\n- **Free-market economy**\n\nIn practice, actual economies are **mixed economies** containing elements of all three. \n\nGovernment may also:\n- Protect private property.\n- Enforce contracts.\n- Correct market failures.\n- Provide public goods.\n- Address externalities.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]}],"deck":"sources/microeconomics/chapter-1.pdf"},{"id":2,"title":"Economic Theories, Data, and Graphs","topics":[{"id":"micro-c2-t1","title":"Positive Statement","body":"A statement about **what is, was, or will be**.\n\n- Based on facts.\n- Can be tested using evidence.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t2","title":"Normative Statement","body":"A statement about **what ought to be**.\n\n- Based on value judgments.\n- Cannot be settled using facts alone.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t3","title":"2.2 Building and Testing Economic Theories","body":"An economic theory is an **abstraction from reality** containing:\n- Variables.\n- Assumptions.\n- Predictions.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t4","title":"Endogenous Variable","body":"A variable explained **within** the theory.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t5","title":"Exogenous Variable","body":"A variable determined **outside** the theory.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t6","title":"Testing Theories","body":"Economists compare a theory's predictions with real-world evidence.\n\nIf the evidence conflicts with the theory, it may be:\n- Modified.\n- Replaced by a better theory.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t7","title":"Positive Correlation","body":"Two variables move in the **same direction**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch2-page20.png","alt":"Positive Correlation: course diagram from Chapter 2, page 20","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 2, page 20"}]},{"id":"micro-c2-t8","title":"Negative Correlation","body":"Two variables move in **opposite directions**.\n\n\nA correlation does **not automatically prove causation**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch2-page20.png","alt":"Negative Correlation: course diagram from Chapter 2, page 20","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 2, page 20"}]},{"id":"micro-c2-t9","title":"Index Numbers","body":"An index number measures a variable relative to a **base period**, which is normally assigned a value of 100. \n\n\\[\n\\text{Index}=\n\\frac{\\text{Value in given period}}\n{\\text{Value in base period}}\\times100\n\\]\n\nExample:\n\nIf production rises from 200 to 250:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{250}{200}\\times100=125\n\\]\n\nThe variable is therefore **25% higher than in the base period**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t10","title":"Consumer Price Index — CPI","body":"The CPI measures changes in the prices consumers pay for a typical basket of goods and services.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t11","title":"Cross-Sectional Data","body":"Data for several different units at the same point in time.\n\nExample:\n- House prices across Canadian provinces in one year.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch2-page16.png","alt":"Cross-Sectional Data: course diagram from Chapter 2, page 16","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 2, page 16"}]},{"id":"micro-c2-t12","title":"Time-Series Data","body":"Observations of the same variable over several periods.\n\nExample:\n- Canada's unemployment rate over many years.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch2-page17.png","alt":"Time-Series Data: course diagram from Chapter 2, page 17","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 2, page 17"}]},{"id":"micro-c2-t13","title":"Scatter Diagram","body":"Plots two variables together, with each point representing one observation.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t14","title":"Functions","body":"When each value of X corresponds to one value of Y:\n\n\\[\nY=f(X)\n\\]\n\nExample from the slides:\n\n\\[\nC=800+0.8W\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(C\\) = consumption.\n- \\(W\\) = wage income.\n\nConsumption rises by $0.80 for every additional $1 of wage income.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch2-page19.png","alt":"Functions: course diagram from Chapter 2, page 19","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 2, page 19"}]},{"id":"micro-c2-t15","title":"Linear","body":"A straight-line relationship with a constant slope.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t16","title":"Non-Linear","body":"A curved relationship where the slope changes.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c2-t17","title":"Slope","body":"\\[\n\\text{Slope}=\\frac{\\Delta Y}{\\Delta X}\n\\]\n\nSlope represents the **marginal response** of one variable to a change in another.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch2-page21.png","alt":"Slope: course diagram from Chapter 2, page 21","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 2, page 21"}]},{"id":"micro-c2-t18","title":"Diminishing Marginal Response","body":"Each additional increase in X produces a smaller response in Y.\n\n\nThe slides explain that, with non-linear functions, the slope changes as X changes.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch2-page22.png","alt":"Diminishing Marginal Response: course diagram from Chapter 2, page 22","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 2, page 22"}]},{"id":"micro-c2-t19","title":"Increasing Marginal Cost","body":"Producing additional output becomes increasingly costly.\n\n\nThe Chapter 2 slides use this as an example of a non-linear economic relationship.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch2-page23.png","alt":"Increasing Marginal Cost: course diagram from Chapter 2, page 23","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 2, page 23"}]}],"deck":"sources/microeconomics/chapter-2.pdf"},{"id":3,"title":"Demand, Supply and Price","topics":[{"id":"micro-c3-t1","title":"Quantity Demanded","body":"The amount consumers desire to purchase during a particular time period.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c3-t2","title":"Law of Demand","body":"**Ceteris paribus:**\n\n\\[\nP\\uparrow \\Rightarrow Q_d\\downarrow\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP\\downarrow \\Rightarrow Q_d\\uparrow\n\\]\n\nPrice and quantity demanded are negatively related.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch3-page5.png","alt":"Law of Demand: course diagram from Chapter 3, page 5","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 3, page 5"}]},{"id":"micro-c3-t3","title":"Demand Shifters","body":"The entire demand curve can shift because of changes in:\n- Consumer income.\n- Prices of other goods.\n- Preferences.\n- Population.\n- Significant weather changes.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c3-t4","title":"Increase in Demand","body":"An increase in demand shifts the curve **rightward**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch3-page7.png","alt":"Increase in Demand: course diagram from Chapter 3, page 7","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 3, page 7"}]},{"id":"micro-c3-t5","title":"Change in Demand vs. Quantity Demanded","body":"**Change in demand**\n- Entire demand curve shifts.\n- Caused by a non-price determinant.\n\n**Change in quantity demanded**\n- Movement along the same demand curve.\n- Caused by a change in the product's own price.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch3-page9.png","alt":"Change in Demand vs. Quantity Demanded: course diagram from Chapter 3, page 9","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 3, page 9"}]},{"id":"micro-c3-t6","title":"Quantity Supplied","body":"The amount firms are willing to offer for sale during a particular period.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c3-t7","title":"Law of Supply","body":"\\[\nP\\uparrow \\Rightarrow Q_s\\uparrow\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP\\downarrow \\Rightarrow Q_s\\downarrow\n\\]\n\nHigher prices generally make production more profitable.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch3-page12.png","alt":"Law of Supply: course diagram from Chapter 3, page 12","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 3, page 12"}]},{"id":"micro-c3-t8","title":"Supply Shifters","body":"Supply may change because of:\n- Input prices.\n- Technology.\n- Government taxes or subsidies.\n- Prices of other products.\n- Weather.\n- Number of suppliers.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c3-t9","title":"Increase in Supply","body":"An increase in supply shifts the curve **rightward**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch3-page14.png","alt":"Increase in Supply: course diagram from Chapter 3, page 14","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 3, page 14"}]},{"id":"micro-c3-t10","title":"Change in Supply vs. Quantity Supplied","body":"**Change in supply**\n- Entire curve shifts.\n\n**Change in quantity supplied**\n- Movement along an existing supply curve because the product's own price changes.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c3-t11","title":"3.3 Market Equilibrium","body":"Equilibrium occurs where:\n\n\\[\nQ_d=Q_s\n\\]\n\nAt equilibrium, the quantity consumers want to buy equals the quantity producers want to sell.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch3-page18.png","alt":"3.3 Market Equilibrium: course diagram from Chapter 3, page 18","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 3, page 18"}]},{"id":"micro-c3-t12","title":"Excess Supply","body":"Occurs when:\n\n\\[\nQ_s>Q_d\n\\]\n\nUsually occurs when price is **above equilibrium**.\n\nResult:\n- Downward pressure on price.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c3-t13","title":"Excess Demand","body":"Occurs when:\n\n\\[\nQ_d>Q_s\n\\]\n\nUsually occurs when price is **below equilibrium**.\n\nResult:\n- Upward pressure on price.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c3-t14","title":"Changes in Equilibrium","body":"| Market change | Equilibrium price | Equilibrium quantity |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Demand increases | ↑ | ↑ |\n| Demand decreases | ↓ | ↓ |\n| Supply increases | ↓ | ↑ |\n| Supply decreases | ↑ | ↓ |","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch3-page20.png","alt":"Changes in Equilibrium: course diagram from Chapter 3, page 20","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 3, page 20"}]},{"id":"micro-c3-t15","title":"Relative and Absolute Prices","body":"**Absolute price:** money required to purchase one unit.\n\n**Relative price:** price of one good expressed in terms of another good.\n\nDemand and supply curves are interpreted using relative prices.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]}],"deck":"sources/microeconomics/chapter-3.pdf"},{"id":4,"title":"Elasticity","topics":[{"id":"micro-c4-t1","title":"4.1 Price Elasticity of Demand","body":"Elasticity measures the **responsiveness of quantity demanded to price changes**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t2","title":"Elastic Demand","body":"Quantity demanded responds strongly to a price change.\n\n\\[\n\\eta_d>1\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t3","title":"Inelastic Demand","body":"Quantity demanded responds relatively little.\n\n\\[\n\\eta_d<1\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t4","title":"Unit Elastic","body":"\\[\n\\eta_d=1\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t5","title":"Price Elasticity Formula","body":"\\[\n\\eta_d=\n\\frac{\\%\\Delta Q_d}\n{\\%\\Delta P}\n\\]\n\nDemand elasticity is normally reported as a positive number even though price and quantity demanded move in opposite directions.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t6","title":"Elasticity Along a Linear Demand Curve","body":"A straight demand curve has a **constant slope but not constant elasticity**. \n\n\nGenerally:\n- Upper portion → elastic.\n- Middle → unit elastic.\n- Lower portion → inelastic.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch4-page8.png","alt":"Elasticity Along a Linear Demand Curve: course diagram from Chapter 4, page 8","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 4, page 8"}]},{"id":"micro-c4-t7","title":"Perfectly Inelastic Demand","body":"\\[\n\\eta=0\n\\]\n\nQuantity demanded does not change when price changes.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch4-page9.png","alt":"Perfectly Inelastic Demand: course diagram from Chapter 4, page 9","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 4, page 9"}]},{"id":"micro-c4-t8","title":"Perfectly Elastic Demand","body":"Quantity demanded is infinitely responsive at one price.\n\n\\[\n\\eta=\\infty\n\\]\n\n\nThe slides compare perfectly inelastic, perfectly elastic and unit-elastic demand.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch4-page9.png","alt":"Perfectly Elastic Demand: course diagram from Chapter 4, page 9","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 4, page 9"}]},{"id":"micro-c4-t9","title":"Availability of Substitutes","body":"More substitutes → demand tends to be **more elastic**.\n\nFewer substitutes → demand tends to be **more inelastic**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t10","title":"Importance in Consumer Budget","body":"Large share of budget → more elastic.\n\nSmall share of budget → more inelastic.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t11","title":"Time","body":"Demand tends to become more elastic over longer periods because consumers have more time to adjust or find substitutes.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t12","title":"Elasticity and Total Expenditure","body":"\\[\nTE=P\\times Q\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t13","title":"Elasticity and Total Expenditure — When Price Falls","body":"| Demand | Total expenditure |\n|---|---|\n| Elastic | Rises |\n| Inelastic | Falls |\n| Unit elastic | Unchanged |","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t14","title":"Elasticity and Total Expenditure — When Price Rises","body":"| Demand | Total expenditure |\n|---|---|\n| Elastic | Falls |\n| Inelastic | Rises |\n| Unit elastic | Unchanged |","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t15","title":"4.2 Price Elasticity of Supply","body":"Measures the responsiveness of quantity supplied to price.\n\n\\[\n\\eta_s=\n\\frac{\\%\\Delta Q_s}{\\%\\Delta P}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t16","title":"4.2 Price Elasticity of Supply — Determinants","body":"Supply is generally more elastic when:\n- Producers can easily shift resources.\n- Producers have more time to adjust productive capacity.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t17","title":"4.3 Excise Taxes","body":"An **excise tax** is a tax on the sale of a particular product.\n\nIt:\n- Raises the price paid by consumers.\n- Lowers the price received by producers.\n- Reduces quantity exchanged.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch4-page24.png","alt":"4.3 Excise Taxes: course diagram from Chapter 4, page 24","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 4, page 24"}]},{"id":"micro-c4-t18","title":"Tax Incidence","body":"**Tax incidence** refers to who actually bears the burden of a tax.\n\nThe burden depends on the relative elasticities of supply and demand. \n\n**Rule to remember:**\n\n> The more inelastic side of the market tends to bear more of the tax burden.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch4-page26.png","alt":"Tax Incidence: course diagram from Chapter 4, page 26","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 4, page 26"}]},{"id":"micro-c4-t19","title":"4.4 Income Elasticity of Demand","body":"\\[\n\\eta_Y=\n\\frac{\\%\\Delta Q_d}\n{\\%\\Delta Income}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t20","title":"Normal Good","body":"\\[\n\\eta_Y>0\n\\]\n\nDemand rises as income rises.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t21","title":"Inferior Good","body":"\\[\n\\eta_Y<0\n\\]\n\nDemand falls as income rises.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t22","title":"Necessity","body":"\\[\n0<\\eta_Y<1\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t23","title":"Luxury","body":"\\[\n\\eta_Y>1\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t24","title":"Cross-Price Elasticity","body":"\\[\n\\eta_{XY}=\n\\frac{\\%\\Delta Q_X}\n{\\%\\Delta P_Y}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t25","title":"Substitutes","body":"\\[\n\\eta_{XY}>0\n\\]\n\nIf the price of Y rises, demand for X rises.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c4-t26","title":"Complements","body":"\\[\n\\eta_{XY}<0\n\\]\n\nIf the price of Y rises, demand for X falls.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]}],"deck":"sources/microeconomics/chapter-4.pdf"},{"id":5,"title":"Price Controls and Market Efficiency","topics":[{"id":"micro-c5-t1","title":"Disequilibrium","body":"When the market is not at equilibrium, actual quantity exchanged is determined by the **smaller of quantity demanded and quantity supplied**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t2","title":"Price Floor","body":"A **price floor** is the minimum legal price that may be charged.\n\nA binding price floor is set **above equilibrium**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page5.png","alt":"Price Floor: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 5","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 5"}]},{"id":"micro-c5-t3","title":"Price Floor — Result","body":"\\[\nQ_s>Q_d\n\\]\n\nThis creates **excess supply**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t4","title":"Minimum Wage","body":"A minimum wage is a price floor in the labour market.\n\nA binding minimum wage in a competitive labour market:\n- Reduces employment.\n- Increases quantity of labour supplied.\n- Increases unemployment.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t5","title":"Price Ceiling","body":"A **price ceiling** is the maximum legal price.\n\nA binding price ceiling is below equilibrium.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page9.png","alt":"Price Ceiling: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 9","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 9"}]},{"id":"micro-c5-t6","title":"Price Ceiling — Result","body":"\\[\nQ_d>Q_s\n\\]\n\nThis creates:\n- Excess demand.\n- A shortage.\n- Possible queues or other allocation methods.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t7","title":"Hidden Markets","body":"A hidden market occurs when products are sold at prices that violate a legal price control.\n\nSomeone may:\n- Buy at the legal controlled price.\n- Resell at a higher illegal price.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t8","title":"5.2 Rent Controls","body":"Rent controls are a form of **price ceiling**.\n\nA binding rent control leads to:\n- Rental housing shortages.\n- Alternative allocation methods.\n- Hidden markets.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t9","title":"Short Run vs. Long Run","body":"In the short run:\n- Housing supply is relatively fixed.\n\nIn the long run:\n- Rental supply may shrink.\n- The shortage becomes larger.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page12.png","alt":"Short Run vs. Long Run: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 12","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 12"}]},{"id":"micro-c5-t10","title":"Short Run vs. Long Run — Who Gains?","body":"Existing tenants in controlled units.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t11","title":"Short Run vs. Long Run — Who Loses?","body":"Landlords.\n- Potential future tenants.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t12","title":"Short Run vs. Long Run — Alternatives","body":"Government may:\n- Subsidize housing construction.\n- Build public housing.\n- Provide income assistance.\n\nEach option uses resources and therefore has a cost.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c5-t13","title":"Demand as Value","body":"The demand curve shows the **maximum price consumers are willing to pay** for each unit.\n\nIts height represents the value consumers place on that unit.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page19.png","alt":"Demand as Value: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 19","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 19"}]},{"id":"micro-c5-t14","title":"Supply as Cost","body":"The supply curve shows the **minimum price producers are willing to accept**.\n\nIts height represents the additional cost of producing each unit.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Revised chapter notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page21.png","alt":"Supply as Cost: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 21","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 21"}]},{"id":"micro-c5-t15","title":"Economic Surplus and Market Efficiency","body":"**Economic surplus** is the total value consumers place on the units exchanged minus their total production cost. On a demand–supply graph, it is the area below demand and above supply up to the quantity traded.\n\nIn the competitive market model used in these slides, total surplus is maximized at the free-market equilibrium quantity.","format":"markdown","source":"Added from Chapter 5 course slides, page 22 · Conversation preview ends before this section","sourcePage":22,"figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page22.png","alt":"Economic Surplus and Market Efficiency: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 22","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 22"}]},{"id":"micro-c5-t16","title":"Price Controls and Deadweight Loss","body":"Binding price floors and ceilings reduce the quantity traded below the competitive equilibrium. Some mutually beneficial trades no longer occur.\n\n**Deadweight loss** is the resulting loss of total economic surplus to society. The slides show it as the area between demand and supply over the units that are no longer exchanged.","format":"markdown","source":"Added from Chapter 5 course slides, page 23 · Conversation preview ends before this section","sourcePage":23,"figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page23.png","alt":"Price Controls and Deadweight Loss: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 23","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 23"},{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page24.png","alt":"Price Controls and Deadweight Loss: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 24","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 24"}]},{"id":"micro-c5-t17","title":"Output Quotas","body":"An **output quota** limits how much firms may produce. A binding quota below the competitive equilibrium quantity reduces output and raises the market price.\n\nThe lost gains from the units no longer traded create deadweight loss.","format":"markdown","source":"Added from Chapter 5 course slides, page 25 · Conversation preview ends before this section","sourcePage":25,"figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch5-page25.png","alt":"Output Quotas: course diagram from Chapter 5, page 25","caption":"Original course slide · Chapter 5, page 25"}]},{"id":"micro-c5-t18","title":"Efficiency and Policy Judgements","body":"Governments may accept a loss of economic surplus to help a particular group.\n\n**Positive analysis** examines a policy’s actual effects. **Normative judgements** concern whether those effects are desirable. Economic surplus is one consideration in a policy decision; it does not settle distributional or social goals.","format":"markdown","source":"Added from Chapter 5 course slides, page 26 · Conversation preview ends before this section","sourcePage":26,"figures":[]}],"deck":"sources/microeconomics/chapter-5.pdf"},{"id":7,"title":"Producers in the Short Run","topics":[{"id":"micro-c7-t1","title":"Chapter overview","body":"The main things Chapter 7 wants you to know are firm organization/financing, accounting vs. economic profit, short-run production, diminishing marginal returns, and short-run costs.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t2","title":"7.1 What Are Firms?","body":"### Forms of business organization\n\n| Type | Main idea |\n|---|---|\n| Single proprietorship | One owner-manager; personally responsible for debts |\n| Ordinary partnership | 2+ owners; partners personally responsible for debts |\n| Limited partnership | General partners run firm and have full liability; limited partners have liability limited to investment |\n| Corporation | Separate legal identity; owners generally not personally responsible for firm's actions |\n| State-owned enterprise | Government owned; called a **Crown corporation** in Canada |\n| Non-profit organization | Provides goods/services; profits stay in organization |\n\n    \n\n### Multinational enterprise\n\nA firm operating in more than one country is a:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Multinational Enterprise (MNE)}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t3","title":"Financing Firms","body":"### Financial capital vs. physical capital\n\n**Financial capital** = money raised to finance the business.\n\n**Physical capital** = productive assets such as machinery, factories, offices, and vehicles. \n\nThe two main sources of financial capital are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Equity and Debt}}\n\\]\n\n### Equity\n\nEquity means **ownership**.\n\nCorporations raise equity by selling:\n\n- shares\n- stocks\n- equities\n\nProfits paid to shareholders are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Dividends}}\n\\]\n\n### Debt\n\nDebt means the lender is a **creditor, not an owner**.\n\nThe firm must repay:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Principal + Interest}}\n\\]\n\nExamples include bank loans and bonds.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t4","title":"Production Function","body":"The production function shows the **maximum output** obtainable from a given combination of inputs:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Q=f(L,K)}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- \\(Q\\) = output\n- \\(L\\) = labour\n- \\(K\\) = capital","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t5","title":"Accounting Profit vs. Economic Profit","body":"This is very important.\n\n### Explicit costs\n\nCosts involving resources used by the firm, such as:\n\n- wages\n- rent\n- interest\n- intermediate inputs\n- depreciation\n\nAccounting profit:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Accounting Profit}\n=\\text{Revenue}-\\text{Explicit Costs}}\n\\]\n\n### Implicit costs\n\nImplicit costs do **not necessarily involve a market transaction**, but they have an opportunity cost.\n\nExamples:\n\n- owner's time\n- owner's invested capital\n\n### Economic profit\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{Economic Profit}\n=\n\\text{Revenue}\n-\n(\\text{Explicit Costs}+\\text{Implicit Costs})\n}\n\\]\n\nor:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{Economic Profit}\n=\n\\text{Accounting Profit}-\\text{Implicit Costs}\n}\n\\]\n\n### Important relationship\n\nBecause economic profit subtracts **more costs**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Economic Profit}\\leq\\text{Accounting Profit}}\n\\]\n\nExample from the slides:\n\nAccounting profit:\n\n\\[\n840\n\\]\n\nImplicit costs:\n\n\\[\n265\n\\]\n\nEconomic profit:\n\n\\[\n840-265=\\boxed{575}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t6","title":"Profit Formula","body":"When the chapter refers to a firm's profit, it means **economic profit**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi=TR-TC}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- \\(\\pi\\) = economic profit\n- \\(TR\\) = total revenue\n- \\(TC\\) = total economic cost","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t7","title":"Short Run vs. Long Run vs. Very Long Run","body":"| Period | What can change? |\n|---|---|\n| Short run | At least one factor is fixed |\n| Long run | All factors can vary; technology fixed |\n| Very long run | Inputs **and technology** can vary |\n\nThe terms do **not** refer to a specific number of months or years.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t8","title":"Total Product","body":"\\[\n\\boxed{TP=\\text{total output produced}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t9","title":"Average Product","body":"\\[\n\\boxed{AP=\\frac{TP}{L}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(L\\) is labour.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t10","title":"Marginal Product","body":"Marginal product measures the additional output produced by an additional unit of labour:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP=\\frac{\\Delta TP}{\\Delta L}}\n\\]\n\n \n\n### Example\n\nSuppose:\n\n\\[\nL: 5\\rightarrow6\n\\]\n\nand:\n\n\\[\nTP:35\\rightarrow55\n\\]\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\nMP=\\frac{55-35}{6-5}=20\n\\]\n\nSo the sixth worker adds:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{20\\text{ units}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t11","title":"Total Product Graph","body":"The slide's production table shows TP increasing from 0 to 120 as labour rises from 0 to 13 workers. Initially TP rises at an increasing rate, then eventually rises at a decreasing rate.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch7-total-product.svg","alt":"Total product rises first at an increasing rate, then at a decreasing rate.","caption":"Recreated from the chapter relationships · Illustrative schematic, not original slide data"}]},{"id":"micro-c7-t12","title":"Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns","body":"This is one of the biggest Chapter 7 concepts.\n\nIn the short run:\n\n- at least one factor is fixed\n- more units of a variable factor are added\n- eventually each additional unit adds **less output than the previous one**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Eventually }MP\\downarrow}\n\\]\n\nWhy?\n\nEach new worker has less of the fixed factor—such as machinery or workspace—to work with. \n\n### Important\n\nDiminishing marginal returns does **not** mean total output immediately falls.\n\nIt means:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{TP can still rise, but at a decreasing rate}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t13","title":"Relationship Between MP and AP","body":"This relationship is extremely testable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP>AP\\Rightarrow AP\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP<AP\\Rightarrow AP\\downarrow}\n\\]\n\nand:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP=AP\\text{ at maximum AP}}\n\\]\n\nThe MP curve crosses AP exactly where AP reaches its maximum. \n\n### Memory trick\n\nThink of your grade average.\n\nIf your next grade is **above your average**, your average rises.\n\nIf your next grade is **below your average**, your average falls.\n\nMarginal product works the same way.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t14","title":"AP and MP Graph","body":"Notice that MP reaches its peak first and then begins falling. AP keeps rising until MP crosses it.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch7-ap-mp.svg","alt":"Marginal product crosses average product at the maximum of average product.","caption":"Recreated from the chapter relationships · Illustrative schematic, not original slide data"}]},{"id":"micro-c7-t15","title":"7.4 Short-Run Costs","body":"The basic cost relationship is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{TC=TFC+TVC}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- \\(TC\\) = total cost\n- \\(TFC\\) = total fixed cost\n- \\(TVC\\) = total variable cost\n\nAverage total cost:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ATC=AFC+AVC}\n\\]\n\nUseful formulas:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AFC=\\frac{TFC}{Q}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AVC=\\frac{TVC}{Q}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ATC=\\frac{TC}{Q}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t16","title":"Marginal Cost","body":"Marginal cost is the increase in total cost caused by producing additional output:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC=\\frac{\\Delta TC}{\\Delta Q}}\n\\]\n\nBecause fixed costs do not change as output changes:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta TC=\\Delta TVC}\n\\]\n\nso marginal cost comes from changes in variable cost.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t17","title":"Short-Run Cost Curve Relationships","body":"Key rules:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC<ATC\\Rightarrow ATC\\downarrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC>ATC\\Rightarrow ATC\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC=ATC\\text{ at minimum ATC}}\n\\]\n\nThe same relationship exists with AVC:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC=AVC\\text{ at minimum AVC}}\n\\]\n\nThe slides explicitly note that MC intersects ATC and AVC at their minimum points.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch7-short-run-costs.svg","alt":"Marginal cost crosses AVC and ATC at their respective minima; AFC continually falls.","caption":"Recreated from the chapter relationships · Illustrative schematic, not original slide data"}]},{"id":"micro-c7-t18","title":"Why are MC and AVC U-shaped?","body":"The production and cost curves are connected.\n\nWhen marginal product rises:\n\n\\[\nMP\\uparrow\\Rightarrow MC\\downarrow\n\\]\n\nWhen marginal product falls:\n\n\\[\nMP\\downarrow\\Rightarrow MC\\uparrow\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP\\text{ maximum}\\Longleftrightarrow MC\\text{ minimum}}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ maximum}\\Longleftrightarrow AVC\\text{ minimum}}\n\\]\n\nThese inverse production–cost relationships assume labour is the only variable input and its wage is constant.\n\nThis is very useful for multiple-choice questions.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t19","title":"AFC Behaviour","body":"Because fixed cost stays constant while output increases:\n\n\\[\nAFC=\\frac{TFC}{Q}\n\\]\n\ntherefore:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Q\\uparrow\\Rightarrow AFC\\downarrow}\n\\]\n\nAFC continually falls as output increases.\n\nIt does **not** turn upward.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t20","title":"Capacity","body":"The firm's **capacity** is the output at which short-run average total cost is minimized:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Capacity}=\\text{output at minimum ATC}}\n\\]\n\nIf a firm produces less than that quantity:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Excess capacity}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c7-t21","title":"Cost Curve Shifts","body":"This is another good exam topic.\n\n### Variable-factor price increases\n\nExample: wage increases.\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\nand:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ATC\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\nbecause producing each additional unit is more expensive. \n\n### Fixed-factor price increases\n\nExample: higher building rent that does not depend on output.\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ATC\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\nbut:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC\\text{ unchanged}}\n\\]\n\nbecause fixed costs don't change when one extra unit is produced. \n\n### Great cheat-sheet table\n\n| Cost change | ATC | MC |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Variable input price ↑ | ↑ | ↑ |\n| Fixed input price ↑ | ↑ | **No change** |","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch7-variable-cost-shift.svg","alt":"A higher wage raises marginal cost and average total cost at each output.","caption":"Recreated from the chapter relationships · Illustrative schematic, not original slide data"}]},{"id":"micro-c7-t22","title":"Chapter 7 — Formula Box","body":"\\[\n\\boxed{Q=f(L,K)}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Accounting Profit}=TR-\\text{Explicit Costs}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Economic Profit}=TR-(Explicit+Implicit)}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi=TR-TC}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP=\\frac{TP}{L}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP=\\frac{\\Delta TP}{\\Delta L}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{TC=TFC+TVC}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ATC=AFC+AVC}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AFC=\\frac{TFC}{Q},\\quad AVC=\\frac{TVC}{Q},\\quad ATC=\\frac{TC}{Q}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC=\\frac{\\Delta TC}{\\Delta Q}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]}]},{"id":8,"title":"Producers in the Long Run","topics":[{"id":"micro-c8-t1","title":"Chapter overview","body":"Chapter 8 moves from a situation where some inputs are fixed to one where **all inputs can be changed**. The main topics are cost minimization, input substitution, LRAC, economies/diseconomies of scale, the relationship between LRAC and SRATC, and technological change.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t2","title":"8.1 Long Run: No Fixed Factors","body":"Remember:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Short run: some inputs fixed}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Long run: all inputs variable}}\n\\]\n\nIn the long run, firms can choose between many different combinations of labour and capital.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t3","title":"Technical Efficiency","body":"A production method is **technically efficient** if inputs are combined so as to maximize output.\n\nA technique is clearly inefficient if another technique can produce the **same output using less of at least one input and no more of the others**.\n\nFor example, the slide compares techniques for producing 1,000 widgets. Technique D uses 30 labour and 60 capital while A uses only 25 labour and 50 capital for the same output, so D is obviously dominated. \n\nBut:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Technical efficiency does NOT automatically mean lowest cost}}\n\\]\n\nThe firm must choose the technically efficient method that has the **lowest monetary cost**.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t4","title":"Cost Minimization","body":"Profit maximization implies the firm should produce any output at the **lowest possible cost**. \n\nSuppose:\n\n- \\(MP_L\\) = marginal product of labour\n- \\(MP_K\\) = marginal product of capital\n- \\(p_L\\) = price of labour\n- \\(p_K\\) = price of capital\n\nCost minimization requires:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}\n=\n\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}\n}\n\\]\n\nEquivalent form:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\frac{MP_K}{MP_L}\n=\n\\frac{p_K}{p_L}\n}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t5","title":"How to Interpret MP/Price","body":"Think of:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{MP}{P_{\\text{input}}}}\n\\]\n\nas:\n\n> **extra output per dollar spent on that input**\n\nSuppose:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}=2\n\\]\n\nand:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}=5\n\\]\n\nCapital gives more output per dollar.\n\nTherefore the firm should:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{use more capital and less labour}}\n\\]\n\nuntil the two ratios become equal.\n\n### Rule\n\nIf:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}>\n\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}\n\\]\n\nuse:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{more labour, less capital}}\n\\]\n\nIf:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}>\n\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}\n\\]\n\nuse:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{more capital, less labour}}\n\\]\n\nAt cost minimization:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}=\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}}\n\\]\n\nThe slides explain that if the ratios are unequal, input substitution can reduce cost.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t6","title":"Principle of Substitution","body":"If the relative price of an input changes:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{use relatively more of the cheaper input}}\n\\]\n\nand:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{use relatively less of the more expensive input}}\n\\]\n\nExample:\n\nIf wages increase while capital price stays unchanged:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Labour becomes relatively expensive}}\n\\]\n\nSo the firm tends to substitute:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Capital for labour}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t7","title":"Long-Run Average Cost","body":"The LRAC curve shows:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{lowest possible average cost at each output when ALL inputs can vary}}\n\\]\n\nSince there are no fixed factors in the long run, the short-run distinction among AFC, AVC, and ATC is not needed in the same way.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t8","title":"LRAC Graph","body":"The LRAC curve in the slides is **saucer-shaped**.\n\nThe three important regions are:\n\n| LRAC behaviour | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Falling LRAC | Economies of scale |\n| Flat/minimum region | Constant returns |\n| Rising LRAC | Diseconomies of scale |","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch8-lrac.svg","alt":"Long-run average cost falls, reaches its minimum at MES, then eventually rises.","caption":"Recreated from the chapter relationships · Illustrative schematic, not original slide data"}]},{"id":"micro-c8-t9","title":"Economies of Scale","body":"When expanding production causes long-run average cost to fall:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Q\\uparrow\\Rightarrow LRAC\\downarrow}\n\\]\n\nThis is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Economies of Scale}}\n\\]\n\nThe slides associate this region with **increasing returns**: output increases more than proportionally to inputs. \n\n### Increasing returns\n\nIf inputs rise by 10% and output rises by **more than 10%**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Increasing returns}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t10","title":"Minimum Efficient Scale","body":"The **minimum efficient scale (MES)** is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{smallest output at which LRAC reaches its minimum}}\n\\]\n\nAt this point, all available economies of scale have been realized. \n\nThis is different from Chapter 7's **capacity**:\n\n- **Capacity** → minimum **short-run ATC**\n- **MES** → smallest output at minimum **LRAC**\n\nThat distinction is worth putting on your cheat sheet.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t11","title":"Constant Returns","body":"Constant returns means:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\%\\Delta Output=\\%\\Delta Inputs}\n\\]\n\nIf all inputs rise 10%, output rises 10%.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t12","title":"Diseconomies of Scale / Decreasing Returns","body":"Past the low-cost range, LRAC begins rising.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Q\\uparrow\\Rightarrow LRAC\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\nThis is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Diseconomies of Scale}}\n\\]\n\nThe slides connect this with **decreasing returns**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\%\\Delta Output<\\%\\Delta Inputs}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t13","title":"LRAC vs. SRATC","body":"This is probably one of the most important Chapter 8 graph topics.\n\n### SRATC\n\nShort-run average total cost:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Some factors are fixed}}\n\\]\n\n### LRAC\n\nLong-run average cost:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All factors are variable}}\n\\]\n\nTherefore LRAC shows the **lowest attainable cost** for each output level. \n\nThis means:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{SRATC\\text{ cannot lie below LRAC}}\n\\]\n\nEach SRATC curve represents a different fixed plant size.\n\nThe SRATC touches the LRAC where that particular plant size is optimal:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{SRATC tangent to LRAC at its optimal output}}\n\\]\n\nAt other outputs:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{SRATC>LRAC}\n\\]\n\n### Exam method\n\nIf a question gives several plant-size SRATC curves:\n\n1. Find the quantity being produced.\n2. Look vertically at all available SRATC curves.\n3. The lowest-cost plant at that quantity is the one closest to/touching LRAC.\n4. If the firm is on a higher SRATC, it could lower cost by changing plant size.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[{"src":"sources/microeconomics/figures/ch8-lrac-sratc.svg","alt":"Short-run plant cost curves touch LRAC from above and cannot lie below it.","caption":"Recreated from the chapter relationships · Illustrative schematic, not original slide data"}]},{"id":"micro-c8-t14","title":"8.2 Very Long Run: Technology","body":"In the **very long run**, technology can change.\n\nTechnological changes shift the LRAC curve. \n\n### Productivity\n\nProductivity means:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Output produced per unit of input}}\n\\]\n\nExamples:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\text{Output}}{\\text{Worker}}}\n\\]\n\nor:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\text{Output}}{\\text{Hour worked}}}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t15","title":"Types of Technological Change","body":"The slides identify three:\n\n1. **New techniques**\n2. **Improved inputs**\n3. **New products**\n\nTechnology is described as **endogenous** because firms deliberately innovate in search of profits.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t16","title":"Substitute Away vs. Innovate Away","body":"If an input becomes more expensive, firms can:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Substitute away from it}}\n\\]\n\nor:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Innovate away from it}}\n\\]\n\nor do both over different time horizons. \n\nExample:\n\nLabour gets expensive.\n\n**Long run:**\n\nUse more machines and less labour.\n\n**Very long run:**\n\nInvent an entirely new automated technology.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t17","title":"Chapter 8 — Formula / Rule Box","body":"\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}\n=\n\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}\n}\n\\]\n\nCost minimizing condition.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}>\n\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{use more labour}\n}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}>\n\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{use more capital}\n}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Falling LRAC}=\\text{Economies of Scale}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{MES}=\\text{smallest output at minimum LRAC}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Rising LRAC}=\\text{Diseconomies of Scale}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{SRATC}\\geq\\text{LRAC}\n}\n\\]","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]},{"id":"micro-c8-t18","title":"Most Important Chapter 7–8 Relationships","body":"For your eventual exam cheat sheet, these are the ones I would prioritize:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP>AP\\Rightarrow AP\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP<AP\\Rightarrow AP\\downarrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP=AP\\text{ at max AP}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP\\downarrow\\Rightarrow MC\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MP\\text{ max}\\Longleftrightarrow MC\\text{ min}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ max}\\Longleftrightarrow AVC\\text{ min}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC<ATC\\Rightarrow ATC\\downarrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC>ATC\\Rightarrow ATC\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{MC=ATC\\text{ at min ATC}}\n\\]\n\nand for Chapter 8:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\frac{MP_L}{p_L}\n=\n\\frac{MP_K}{p_K}\n}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Cheaper input}\\Rightarrow\\text{use relatively more}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Economies of scale}\\Rightarrow LRAC\\downarrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Diseconomies of scale}\\Rightarrow LRAC\\uparrow}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{LRAC is the lowest attainable average cost when all inputs can vary}}\n\\]\n\nThese relationships are much more likely to help with calculation and application questions than memorizing long textbook definitions.","format":"markdown","source":"Create Chapter Notes and Graphs · Chapter 7–8 notes","figures":[]}]}];
window.MICRO_QUIZZES = [{"id":1,"questions":[{"prompt":"Economics is the study of how ______ resources are used to satisfy unlimited human wants.","options":["unlimited","scarce","free","renewable"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Which term refers to natural resources used in production?","options":["Labour","Capital","Land","Consumption"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"Physical and mental human effort used in production is called:","options":["labour","capital","land","specialization"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Tools, machinery, and equipment used in production are classified as:","options":["land","services","capital","labour"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"Which statement best describes scarcity?","options":["Everyone has limited wants.","Resources are unlimited.","Resources are limited relative to human wants.","Governments control all resources."],"answer":2},{"prompt":"The value of the next-best alternative that is given up when a choice is made is called:","options":["marginal cost","opportunity cost","total cost","scarcity"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A student chooses to attend university instead of working full-time. The wages the student could have earned are an example of:","options":["capital","productive efficiency","opportunity cost","specialization"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"On a budget line, a point located outside the line is:","options":["efficient","attainable","unattainable","always preferred"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"A point located inside a Production Possibilities Boundary is:","options":["unattainable","efficient","inefficient","impossible to identify"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"A point located directly on a PPB is generally:","options":["unattainable","productively efficient","inefficient","evidence of idle resources"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A point outside the PPB represents:","options":["an attainable but inefficient combination","a currently unattainable combination","perfect efficiency","idle resources"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The PPB illustrates all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["scarcity","choice","opportunity cost","unlimited production"],"answer":3},{"prompt":"If an economy experiences economic growth, its PPB will generally:","options":["shift inward","shift outward","become vertical","disappear"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"An economy operating inside its PPB most likely has:","options":["idle resources","unlimited resources","maximum efficiency","no opportunity cost"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Which of the following is mainly a microeconomic question?","options":["What determines Canada's total unemployment rate?","What determines the price of coffee?","What determines national economic growth?","What determines total national output?"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Macroeconomics studies economic aggregates such as:","options":["the price charged by one restaurant","an individual's shopping choices","total output and employment","the cost of producing one product"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"A market economy is described as self-organizing because:","options":["the government makes every decision","consumers and producers make decentralized decisions that become coordinated","all firms cooperate directly","every individual has identical preferences"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If the price of a product rises, sellers are generally given an incentive to:","options":["sell less","stop producing","sell more","reduce demand"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the three major types of decision makers in an economy?","options":["Weather systems","Consumers","Natural resources","Technology"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The specialization of individual workers in producing particular goods and services is called:","options":["globalization","specialization of labour","opportunity cost","resource idleness"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Breaking production into a sequence of specialized tasks is known as:","options":["division of labour","market failure","economic growth","scarcity"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Why does money make specialization easier?","options":["It eliminates scarcity.","It removes the need for production.","It makes trade easier by avoiding the difficulties of barter.","It guarantees equal incomes."],"answer":2},{"prompt":"One major cause of globalization identified in the notes is:","options":["rising transportation costs","reduced information technology","falling transportation costs","declining international trade"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three pure types of economic systems?","options":["Traditional","Command","Free-market","Inflationary"],"answer":3},{"prompt":"Canada would best be described as a mixed economy because:","options":["it uses only central planning","it contains elements of markets, government intervention, and other forms of organization","it has no private property","government produces every good and service"],"answer":1}],"source":"Questions from Create Chapter Notes and Graphs."},{"id":2,"questions":[{"prompt":"A statement describing what actually is, was, or will be is called a:","options":["normative statement","positive statement","value judgment","policy recommendation"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A statement about what ought to happen is called:","options":["positive","empirical","normative","endogenous"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"\"The government should increase the minimum wage\" is primarily:","options":["a normative statement","a positive statement","an index number","a function"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"\"An increase in the price of a product reduces its quantity demanded, other things equal\" is primarily:","options":["normative","positive","moral","subjective"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"An economic theory is best described as:","options":["a perfect copy of reality","an abstraction from reality","a political opinion","an index number"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Which of the following is normally included in an economic theory?","options":["Variables","Assumptions","Predictions","All of the above"],"answer":3},{"prompt":"A variable whose value is explained within a theory is called:","options":["exogenous","endogenous","normative","indexed"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A variable determined outside the theory is called:","options":["endogenous","dependent","exogenous","marginal"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"An economic theory is tested mainly by:","options":["asking whether people agree with it","comparing its predictions with evidence","determining whether it sounds reasonable","using only value judgments"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If repeated evidence strongly conflicts with a theory, economists may:","options":["ignore the evidence","amend or replace the theory","automatically declare the evidence false","make the theory normative"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If X and Y tend to move in the same direction, they have:","options":["negative correlation","positive correlation","no relationship","diminishing marginal response"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If X rises while Y tends to fall, X and Y have:","options":["positive correlation","negative correlation","unit elasticity","economic efficiency"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Fill in the blank: Correlation does not necessarily prove ______.","options":["scarcity","causation","specialization","slope"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Why can identifying causation in economics be difficult?","options":["Economic variables are often affected by many forces at the same time.","Economists never use data.","There are no economic theories.","Prices never change."],"answer":0},{"prompt":"An index number is usually expressed relative to a base period assigned a value of:","options":["0","1","10","100"],"answer":3},{"prompt":"If production was 200 units in the base year and 250 units this year, the index number is:","options":["80","100","125","150"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"If an index rises from 100 to 120, the variable has increased by:","options":["10%","20%","100%","120%"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The Consumer Price Index is an example of:","options":["an index number","a normative statement","an exogenous variable","a PPB"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"A graph comparing average house prices across Canadian provinces in the same year uses:","options":["time-series data","cross-sectional data","normative data","marginal data"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A graph showing Canada's unemployment rate from 2000 through 2025 uses:","options":["cross-sectional data","time-series data","opportunity-cost data","normative data"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A scatter diagram is used to:","options":["show two variables with each point representing an observation","calculate opportunity cost only","show only one variable over time","distinguish land from labour"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"If Y=f(X), this means:","options":["X must always equal Y","Y is a function of X","Y causes X in every case","X and Y must have negative correlation"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If two variables increase together, they are:","options":["negatively related","positively related","unrelated","perfectly inelastic"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The slope of a straight line is calculated as:","options":["Δ X/Δ Y only","Δ Y/Δ X","X + Y","X × Y"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A curved function becomes flatter as X increases. This is an example of:","options":["increasing marginal response","diminishing marginal response","perfect correlation","an index number"],"answer":1}],"source":"Questions from Create Chapter Notes and Graphs."},{"id":3,"questions":[{"prompt":"Quantity demanded refers to:","options":["the amount consumers desire to buy during a period","the total amount firms produce","the amount government requires consumers to buy","the total amount currently in storage"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"According to the law of demand, ceteris paribus, when price rises:","options":["quantity demanded rises","quantity demanded falls","supply falls","demand shifts right"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Fill in the blank: Price and quantity demanded are normally ______ related.","options":["positively","negatively","perfectly","randomly"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Which event causes a movement along an existing demand curve?","options":["A change in consumer income","A change in population","A change in the product's own price","A change in preferences"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"A change in consumer income causes:","options":["a movement along the demand curve","a shift of the demand curve","movement along the supply curve","no possible market effect"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If demand increases, the demand curve shifts:","options":["left","right","upward along the same curve","downward along the same curve"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A decrease in demand is represented by:","options":["a rightward demand shift","a leftward demand shift","an upward supply shift only","a movement toward equilibrium only"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Which of the following is a demand shifter?","options":["Consumer preferences","Consumer income","Population","All of the above"],"answer":3},{"prompt":"Suppose a popular celebrity promotes a particular brand and consumer preferences for it rise. What happens?","options":["Demand shifts right.","Demand shifts left.","Quantity demanded falls along the original curve.","Supply automatically falls."],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Quantity supplied refers to:","options":["the amount firms are willing to offer for sale","the amount consumers wish to buy","the total population","the amount of goods already consumed"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"According to the law of supply, when the product's price rises:","options":["quantity supplied generally rises","quantity supplied generally falls","demand shifts left","supply shifts left"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Which event causes movement along a supply curve?","options":["A change in technology","A change in input prices","A change in the product's own price","A change in taxes"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"An improvement in production technology will generally:","options":["decrease supply","increase supply","decrease demand","increase quantity demanded along the same curve"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A rise in input costs will most likely:","options":["increase supply","decrease supply","increase demand","create a movement along demand"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If supply decreases, the supply curve shifts:","options":["right","left","downward along itself","nowhere"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If the number of firms producing a product increases, market supply generally:","options":["decreases","increases","becomes zero","has no relationship with the number of suppliers"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Market equilibrium occurs where:","options":["quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied","quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded","quantity demanded equals quantity supplied","price equals zero"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"If the market price is above equilibrium, the market experiences:","options":["excess demand","excess supply","no supply","no demand"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Excess supply creates:","options":["upward pressure on price","downward pressure on price","no pressure on price","automatic demand growth"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If price is below equilibrium, the market experiences:","options":["excess supply","excess demand","equilibrium","no trade"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Excess demand creates:","options":["downward pressure on price","upward pressure on price","a fall in population","a leftward demand shift automatically"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Suppose demand for coffee increases while supply remains unchanged. Equilibrium price and quantity will:","options":["both decrease","both increase","price falls and quantity rises","price rises and quantity falls"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Suppose demand for a product decreases while supply stays the same. What happens?","options":["Equilibrium price and quantity both fall.","Equilibrium price and quantity both rise.","Price rises and quantity falls.","Price falls and quantity rises."],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Suppose supply drops while demand remains unchanged. What happens to equilibrium?","options":["Price falls and quantity rises.","Price rises and quantity falls.","Price and quantity both rise.","Price and quantity both fall."],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Suppose new technology increases supply while demand remains unchanged. What happens?","options":["Equilibrium price rises and quantity falls.","Equilibrium price falls and quantity rises.","Equilibrium price and quantity both fall.","Equilibrium price and quantity both rise."],"answer":1}],"source":"Questions from Create Chapter Notes and Graphs."},{"id":4,"questions":[{"prompt":"Price elasticity of demand measures:","options":["how strongly price responds to taxes","how responsive quantity demanded is to a price change","how responsive supply is to income","how consumers respond to advertising only"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Demand is elastic when:","options":["quantity demanded responds strongly to price changes","quantity demanded does not change at all","price never changes","income always rises"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Demand is inelastic when:","options":["quantity demanded responds strongly to price","quantity demanded responds relatively little to price","elasticity equals infinity","quantity demanded becomes zero"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Price elasticity of demand is calculated as:","options":["% change in price / % change in quantity demanded","% change in quantity demanded / % change in price","price × quantity","quantity supplied / income"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If price elasticity of demand equals 1, demand is:","options":["perfectly elastic","inelastic","unit elastic","perfectly inelastic"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"If elasticity is greater than 1, demand is:","options":["elastic","inelastic","unit elastic","perfectly inelastic"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"If elasticity is less than 1, demand is:","options":["elastic","inelastic","unit elastic","perfectly elastic"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Perfectly inelastic demand has an elasticity of:","options":["0","1","10","infinity"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Perfectly elastic demand is represented graphically by a:","options":["vertical line","horizontal line","downward vertical curve","U-shaped curve"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A straight-line demand curve has:","options":["constant slope and constant elasticity","constant slope but changing elasticity","changing slope but constant elasticity","neither slope nor elasticity"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Products with many close substitutes tend to have:","options":["more elastic demand","more inelastic demand","perfectly inelastic demand","zero demand"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"A narrowly defined product generally has ______ demand than a broadly defined product.","options":["more elastic","less elastic","perfectly inelastic","identical"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"A product taking up a very large portion of someone's budget tends to have:","options":["more elastic demand","more inelastic demand","zero elasticity","no demand curve"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Over longer periods, demand generally becomes:","options":["more elastic","more inelastic","perfectly inelastic","unrelated to substitutes"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Total expenditure is equal to:","options":["price + quantity","price × quantity","price ÷ quantity","quantity − price"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If demand is elastic and price falls, total expenditure will generally:","options":["rise","fall","remain unchanged","become zero"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"If demand is inelastic and price rises, total expenditure will generally:","options":["fall","rise","stay exactly constant","become negative"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If demand is unit elastic and price changes, total expenditure:","options":["rises","falls","remains unchanged","always doubles"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"Price elasticity of supply measures:","options":["responsiveness of quantity supplied to price","responsiveness of quantity demanded to income","responsiveness of price to demand","responsiveness of taxes to government spending"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Supply generally becomes more elastic when producers:","options":["have more time to adjust productive capacity","have less time to respond","cannot move any resources","face permanently fixed output"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"An excise tax is:","options":["a tax on income","a tax on the sale of a particular product","a subsidy","a price ceiling"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"After an excise tax is imposed, the price paid by consumers generally:","options":["falls","rises","becomes zero","must equal the producer's received price"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The economic burden of an excise tax is called:","options":["tax incidence","opportunity cost","income elasticity","economic surplus"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Which side of the market generally bears more of a tax burden?","options":["The more elastic side","The more inelastic side","The side with more firms in every case","Consumers always bear all of it"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If income rises and demand for a product falls, the product is:","options":["normal","inferior","a substitute","a complement"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If income elasticity is positive and greater than 1, the product is generally classified as a:","options":["luxury","necessity","inferior good","complement"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"If income elasticity is positive but less than 1, the product is generally a:","options":["luxury","necessity","substitute","inferior good"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If cross-price elasticity between goods X and Y is positive, the two goods are:","options":["complements","substitutes","inferior goods","necessities"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"If cross-price elasticity between two goods is negative, they are:","options":["substitutes","complements","luxuries","unrelated goods"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The price of tea rises, and consumers respond by buying more coffee. Tea and coffee are most likely:","options":["complements","substitutes","inferior goods","perfectly inelastic goods"],"answer":1}],"source":"Questions from Create Chapter Notes and Graphs. The remaining answer key was checked against the chapter notes because the preview cuts off after answer 13."},{"id":5,"questions":[{"prompt":"In a market away from equilibrium, voluntary quantity exchanged is determined by the ______ of quantity demanded and quantity supplied.","options":["sum","larger","smaller","average"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"A minimum legal price is called a:","options":["price ceiling","price floor","market equilibrium","production subsidy"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"For a price floor to be binding, it must be:","options":["above the equilibrium price","below the equilibrium price","equal to zero","below every seller's cost"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"A binding price floor normally creates:","options":["excess demand","a shortage","excess supply","an outward shift in demand"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"In the competitive labour-market model used in the slides, a binding minimum wage is an example of a:","options":["price floor","price ceiling","tax on goods","quantity subsidy"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Which workers may gain directly from a binding minimum wage in the slides' model?","options":["Workers who lose their jobs","Workers who retain their jobs at the higher wage","All potential workers equally","Only workers outside the labour market"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A maximum legal price is called a:","options":["price floor","price ceiling","market surplus","minimum wage"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A price ceiling is binding when set:","options":["above equilibrium","at any positive price","below equilibrium","above all buyers' willingness to pay"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"At a binding price ceiling, which relationship normally holds?","options":["Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied","Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded","Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied","Demand is zero"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"A market's equilibrium price is $10. A legal ceiling of $15 is introduced. In the basic competitive model, it is:","options":["binding and creates a shortage","non-binding and does not change the equilibrium","binding and creates a surplus","equivalent to a tax of $5"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A market's equilibrium price is $10. A legal floor of $6 is introduced. This floor is:","options":["binding","a price ceiling","non-binding","an output quota"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"At a controlled price, buyers want 100 units while sellers offer 60. How many units can be voluntarily exchanged?","options":["100","160","80","60"],"answer":3},{"prompt":"A binding price ceiling prevents prices from eliminating a shortage. Which alternative allocation method may appear?","options":["Queues","Unlimited output","Automatic equality of all incomes","The disappearance of scarcity"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Products sold at prices that violate a legal price control are traded in a:","options":["perfectly regulated market","hidden market","market with no demand","market with no scarcity"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"A binding rent control is a form of:","options":["price floor","price ceiling","income tax","output subsidy"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Why can rental housing shortages worsen over time under binding rent controls?","options":["Housing supply must become infinite","Producers can adjust and reduce rental supply in the longer run","Every tenant leaves immediately","Rent controls eliminate demand"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The principal gainers identified in the rent-control slides are:","options":["All future tenants","Landlords in every case","Existing tenants in rent-controlled units","All construction firms"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"Which group can lose from a rental shortage caused by binding rent controls?","options":["Potential tenants unable to find accommodation","Every existing tenant equally","Only consumers of unrelated goods","Nobody"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"Which policy alternative in the slides can increase housing availability but uses taxpayer resources?","options":["Eliminating all construction","Subsidizing housing construction","Banning new rental units","Reducing all rental supply"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The height of a demand curve indicates the ______ consumers place on an additional unit.","options":["production cost","tax liability","value","quantity supplied"],"answer":2},{"prompt":"The height of a supply curve represents the additional ______ of producing a unit.","options":["consumer income","cost","consumer surplus","shortage"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"In the competitive model used in the slides, total economic surplus is maximized at:","options":["zero output","the free-market equilibrium quantity","any quantity above equilibrium","any binding price floor"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"The loss of total economic surplus caused by preventing mutually beneficial trades is called:","options":["deadweight loss","income elasticity","a normative statement","excess supply"],"answer":0},{"prompt":"A binding output quota below equilibrium generally causes:","options":["higher output and lower price","lower output, higher price, and deadweight loss","unchanged output and price","unlimited supply"],"answer":1},{"prompt":"Why might policymakers accept a policy that reduces total economic surplus?","options":["Economic surplus is the only policy goal","They may wish to help a particular group and judge the costs worthwhile","The policy cannot have costs","Positive analysis proves every policy is desirable"],"answer":1}],"source":"Supplemental questions created from the attached Chapter 5 slides. The original Chapter 5 quiz is outside the available conversation preview."},{"id":7,"questions":[{"sourceQuestion":1,"prompt":"Which form of business organization has one owner-manager who is personally responsible for the firm's debts?","options":["Corporation","Ordinary partnership","Single proprietorship","Crown corporation"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":2,"prompt":"Two individuals jointly own a business, and both are personally responsible for all its debts. Which organizational structure best describes this business?","options":["Ordinary partnership","Limited partnership with two limited partners","Public corporation","Non-profit organization"],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":3,"prompt":"Which statement correctly describes a limited partner in a limited partnership?","options":["They manage the business and have unlimited liability.","They own publicly traded shares.","They are personally liable for every debt the firm incurs.","They do not manage the business, and their liability is limited to their investment."],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":4,"prompt":"Which type of corporation has shares traded on a stock exchange?","options":["Private corporation","Public corporation","Crown corporation","Ordinary partnership"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":5,"prompt":"A business is owned by the Canadian government and is directed by a state-appointed board. This is most likely a:","options":["Private corporation","Limited partnership","Crown corporation","Single proprietorship"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":6,"prompt":"A corporation raises $500,000 by issuing new shares to investors. This is an example of:","options":["Debt financing","Equity financing","Intermediate-product financing","Depreciation financing"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":7,"prompt":"A company borrows money by issuing bonds. What is the relationship between the bondholders and the firm?","options":["Bondholders are creditors, not owners.","Bondholders automatically become shareholders.","Bondholders become general partners.","Bondholders receive dividends instead of interest."],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":8,"prompt":"Which of the following is an example of physical capital?","options":["A company's bank loan","Cash held for future purchases","Shares issued by a corporation","Machinery used to manufacture products"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":9,"prompt":"Which objective do economists generally assume firms pursue?","options":["Maximizing employee numbers","Maximizing total revenue regardless of cost","Maximizing economic profit","Maximizing the quantity of inputs used"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":10,"prompt":"A pizza restaurant purchases flour from another business to make pizzas. For the restaurant, the flour is:","options":["An intermediate product","A financial asset","A final consumer service","A dividend payment"],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":11,"prompt":"What does the production function Q = f(L, K) describe?","options":["The relationship between price and quantity demanded","The maximum output produced from combinations of labour and capital","The relationship between tax revenue and output","The distribution of profits between owners and creditors"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":12,"prompt":"According to the Chapter 7 slides, depreciation of machinery is classified as:","options":["An implicit cost","An equity payment","A dividend","An explicit cost"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":13,"prompt":"A business owner gives up a job paying $65,000 annually to operate their own company. The forgone salary is:","options":["Total revenue","Depreciation","An implicit cost","A payment of interest"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":14,"prompt":"A firm's total revenue is $950,000 and its explicit costs are $570,000. What is its accounting profit?","options":["$380,000","$1,520,000","$950,000","$570,000"],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":15,"prompt":"A firm earns $1,000,000 in revenue. Its explicit costs are $600,000 and implicit costs are $150,000. What is its economic profit?","options":["$400,000","$250,000","$850,000","$450,000"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":16,"prompt":"A firm earns $900,000 in revenue and incurs $650,000 in explicit costs and $250,000 in implicit costs. What is its economic profit?","options":["$250,000","$650,000","$900,000","$0"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":17,"prompt":"Which situation best represents production in the short run?","options":["A factory cannot change its plant size but can hire more workers.","A firm can change all its inputs and technology.","A firm can change every factor of production with no fixed factors.","A firm must invent new technology to change its output."],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":18,"prompt":"A factory produces 72 units of output using 9 workers. What is the average product of labour?","options":["9 units","7 units","8 units","81 units"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":19,"prompt":"A factory produces 70 units with 5 workers and 88 units with 6 workers. What is the marginal product of the sixth worker?","options":["12 units","14 units","16 units","18 units"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":20,"prompt":"A factory's total product is shown below. Over which interval does marginal product decrease for the first time? Labour: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 | Total product: 0, 4, 11, 19, 25","options":["From 0 to 1 worker","From 3 to 4 workers","From 1 to 2 workers","From 2 to 3 workers"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":21,"prompt":"If marginal product (MP) is greater than average product (AP), what happens to AP as more labour is added?","options":["AP increases.","AP decreases.","AP must remain constant.","Total product must decrease."],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":22,"prompt":"At which point does the marginal product curve intersect the average product curve?","options":["Where marginal product equals zero","Where marginal product is below average product","Where total product is maximized","Where average product is maximized"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":23,"prompt":"A firm has total fixed costs of $150 and total variable costs of $370. What is total cost?","options":["$220","$370","$520","$150"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":24,"prompt":"A firm has fixed costs of $240 and produces 40 units. What is average fixed cost?","options":["$8","$10","$6","$4"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":25,"prompt":"A firm's total variable cost is $330 when it produces 55 units. What is average variable cost?","options":["$6","$3","$275","$18"],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":26,"prompt":"A company produces 35 units at a total cost of $525. What is its average total cost?","options":["$17","$15","$490","$35"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":27,"prompt":"A firm's total cost is $700 when it produces 50 units and $760 when it produces 55 units. What is marginal cost over this output interval?","options":["$60 per unit","$14 per unit","$12 per unit","$10 per unit"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":28,"prompt":"If the price of a variable factor, such as labour, increases, what generally happens to a firm's short-run cost curves?","options":["Only total fixed cost increases.","Average fixed cost increases, but marginal cost stays unchanged.","Average total cost increases, but marginal cost stays unchanged.","Average total cost and marginal cost increase."],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":29,"prompt":"A factory's monthly building rent increases, but rent does not depend on how much it produces. What happens to its short-run cost curves?","options":["ATC increases, but MC stays unchanged.","ATC and MC both increase.","ATC decreases, but MC stays unchanged.","MC increases, but ATC stays unchanged."],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":30,"prompt":"According to Chapter 7, a firm is operating at its capacity output. Which statement is true at the minimum point of its smooth short-run ATC curve?","options":["Average fixed cost reaches its minimum.","Marginal product equals average product.","Average total cost is minimized, and MC intersects ATC.","Total cost equals zero."],"answer":2}],"source":"Questions and answer key from the supplied EC120 Chapter 7 Practice Quiz PDF."},{"id":8,"questions":[{"sourceQuestion":1,"prompt":"A firm can produce 1,000 widgets using any of the following production techniques. Which technique is clearly technically inefficient? Technique A: 25 labour, 50 capital | B: 35 labour, 35 capital C: 50 labour, 25 capital | D: 30 labour, 60 capital","options":["Technique A","Technique B","Technique C","Technique D"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":2,"prompt":"Why does technical efficiency alone not guarantee that a firm is minimizing its costs?","options":["Technically efficient methods always produce different outputs.","Different technically efficient methods can have different costs depending on input prices.","Technically efficient methods always use equal quantities of labour and capital.","Technical efficiency requires all factors to have identical prices."],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":3,"prompt":"Using these techniques for producing 1,000 widgets (A: 25 labour, 50 capital; B: 35 labour, 35 capital; C: 50 labour, 25 capital; D: 30 labour, 60 capital), suppose labour and capital each cost $10 per unit. Which technique minimizes total production cost?","options":["Technique B","Technique A","Technique C","Technique D"],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":4,"prompt":"Using these techniques for producing 1,000 widgets (A: 25 labour, 50 capital; B: 35 labour, 35 capital; C: 50 labour, 25 capital; D: 30 labour, 60 capital), suppose labour costs $10 per unit and capital costs $5 per unit. Which technique minimizes cost?","options":["Technique B","Technique C","Technique A","Technique D"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":5,"prompt":"Using these techniques for producing 1,000 widgets (A: 25 labour, 50 capital; B: 35 labour, 35 capital; C: 50 labour, 25 capital; D: 30 labour, 60 capital), suppose labour costs $5 per unit and capital costs $10 per unit. Which technique minimizes cost?","options":["Technique D","Technique A","Technique B","Technique C"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":6,"prompt":"A company uses 12 units of labour costing $20 each and 4 units of capital costing $50 each. What is total expenditure on these two inputs?","options":["$440","$340","$640","$560"],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":7,"prompt":"A firm has MP of labour = 24 and wage = $8; MP of capital = 36 and capital price = $9. At its current input combination, how should it adjust production to reduce cost while maintaining output?","options":["Use more labour and less capital.","Keep the existing input combination because costs are minimized.","Use more capital and less labour.","Reduce labour and capital by the same percentage."],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":8,"prompt":"Which condition is necessary for a firm to minimize costs using labour and capital at an interior optimum?","options":["MP_L = MP_K","MP_L / p_L = MP_K / p_K","p_L = p_K","L = K"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":9,"prompt":"A firm currently has MP_L / p_L = 5 and MP_K / p_K = 2. Which adjustment moves the firm toward cost minimization, assuming it can substitute inputs while maintaining output?","options":["Increase capital and reduce labour.","Increase both inputs proportionally.","Keep the current combination because the ratios are positive.","Increase labour and reduce capital."],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":10,"prompt":"Suppose wages rise substantially while the price of capital remains unchanged. According to the principle of substitution, what is a firm likely to do in the long run?","options":["Use relatively more labour.","Use relatively more capital.","Use exactly the same input combination regardless of prices.","Stop attempting to minimize production costs."],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":11,"prompt":"Suppose the price of capital decreases while labour costs remain unchanged. What will a cost-minimizing firm generally do?","options":["Substitute relatively more capital for labour.","Substitute relatively more labour for capital.","Reduce the use of capital because it has become cheaper.","Continue with the original input combination regardless of costs."],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":12,"prompt":"A firm has MP_L = 60 and p_L = $20; MP_K = 30 and p_K = $15. What change would move the firm toward cost minimization, assuming it can substitute inputs?","options":["Increase capital and reduce labour.","Increase both factors by the same amount.","Increase labour and reduce capital.","Keep the current combination because marginal products are positive."],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":13,"prompt":"What does the long-run average cost (LRAC) curve represent?","options":["Average cost when all production factors are fixed","The lowest possible average cost for each output level when all inputs can vary","The firm's average fixed cost at every output level","The additional cost of producing one more unit"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":14,"prompt":"Why can a short-run average total cost (SRATC) curve never fall below the LRAC curve for the same technology and factor prices?","options":["SRATC includes no fixed costs.","SRATC always equals marginal cost.","LRAC measures the highest possible production cost.","LRAC represents the lowest attainable cost when all factors can be adjusted."],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":15,"prompt":"What happens where a particular SRATC curve touches the LRAC curve?","options":["That plant size is optimal for producing that output level.","The firm must be producing zero output.","The firm's marginal cost must equal zero.","All possible plant sizes necessarily have equal costs."],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":16,"prompt":"A firm needs to produce 100 units per day. Plant SRATC1 has ATC $12; SRATC2 has ATC $9; SRATC3 has ATC $11. Which plant should a cost-minimizing firm choose?","options":["SRATC1","SRATC3","SRATC2","All three are equally efficient in cost terms."],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":17,"prompt":"A firm's LRAC curve slopes downward as output increases. What is the firm experiencing?","options":["Diseconomies of scale","Increasing average cost","Decreasing returns to scale","Economies of scale"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":18,"prompt":"A firm's LRAC curve rises as output increases. Which concept best explains this?","options":["Economies of scale","Diseconomies of scale","Constant returns to scale","Falling average production costs"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":19,"prompt":"A firm increases all its inputs by 20%, and output also increases by exactly 20%. What is the firm experiencing?","options":["Constant returns to scale","Increasing returns to scale","Decreasing returns to scale","Diminishing marginal product of one fixed input"],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":20,"prompt":"A firm doubles all its inputs, and output triples. Which classification applies?","options":["Decreasing returns to scale","Constant returns to scale","Increasing returns to scale","Negative returns to scale"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":21,"prompt":"A firm increases all its inputs by 50%, but output increases by only 25%. What does this illustrate?","options":["Constant returns to scale","Increasing returns to scale","Economies of scale","Decreasing returns to scale"],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":22,"prompt":"What is the minimum efficient scale (MES) of a firm?","options":["The output where marginal product is zero","The smallest output where LRAC reaches its minimum","The output where total revenue equals total cost","The largest output a firm could physically produce"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":23,"prompt":"A firm has the following LRAC at available outputs: Q=100: $10; Q=200: $8; Q=300: $8; Q=400: $9. What is the smallest output at which it achieves minimum LRAC?","options":["200 units","100 units","300 units","400 units"],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":24,"prompt":"A firm is operating on a SRATC curve that lies above LRAC at its chosen output. What could it do in the long run?","options":["Increase its costs to reach LRAC.","Increase fixed costs without changing any inputs.","Adjust its plant size to produce the same output at lower average cost.","Eliminate the relationship between average cost and output."],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":25,"prompt":"Which statement correctly describes production costs in the long run?","options":["All costs must be fixed.","All factors can vary, so there are no fixed-factor costs in the long-run decision.","Only labour costs can change.","Capital costs cannot change when output changes."],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":26,"prompt":"What distinguishes the very long run from the long run?","options":["Firms have fewer available production techniques.","At least one factor must remain permanently fixed.","Technology is fixed in the very long run but not in the long run.","Technology itself can change in the very long run."],"answer":3},{"sourceQuestion":27,"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three aspects of technological change identified in Chapter 8?","options":["New production techniques","Improved inputs","Changes in financing interest rates alone","New products"],"answer":2},{"sourceQuestion":28,"prompt":"Why do economists describe technological change as endogenous to the economic system?","options":["Firms have incentives to invent and innovate in search of profits.","Technological innovation occurs only because of unpredictable outside events.","Firms have no influence over technological development.","Technology cannot change independently of the size of the labour force."],"answer":0},{"sourceQuestion":29,"prompt":"A firm produces 720 units using 24 workers. What is its labour productivity measured as output per worker?","options":["24 units per worker","30 units per worker","696 units per worker","17,280 units per worker"],"answer":1},{"sourceQuestion":30,"prompt":"Labour becomes more expensive. A firm spends several years developing a new automated production technique that substantially reduces its need for workers. Which response best describes this decision?","options":["Maintaining the original production technique","Increasing the use of labour relative to capital","Moving along an unchanged short-run production function","Innovating away from an expensive input"],"answer":3}],"source":"Questions and answer key from the supplied EC120 Chapter 8 Practice Quiz PDF. Shared production-technique data is repeated where needed so each question stands alone."}];
